  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            In Unit 4's notes you learn that after the verb 'decir' (to say), Spanish always includes the conjunction 'que' when introducing a subordinate clause. \nThe indirect object pronoun 'le' can mean 'to you' (formal) or 'to him/her', and in some areas speakers use 'lo' instead. \nWhen addressing a titled person, Spanish uses the definite article before the title, as in 'El Sr. White'. \nThe command form 'diga' from 'decir' is used to call for someone or something, and 'que' cannot be omitted after forms like 'dijeron'. \nThese conventions ensure clear and polite communication.\n"
    }
  ],
